WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.286
Suppose requests arrive at three hundred jobs
per second for ten seconds, while consumers

00:00:05.286 --> 00:00:10.394
can process two hundred per second. The queue
gains about one thousand jobs during the

00:00:10.394 --> 00:00:15.859
burst. If arrivals then fall to one hundred per
second, the spare processing capacity drains

00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:19.957
that backlog in roughly ten more seconds,
assuming constant job cost.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.140
This is demand smoothing: the work is delayed,
not eliminated. Producers and consumers can

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.280
be sized separately because enqueueing a job is
different work from executing it. However,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.191
the broker must still accept and retain the
backlog safely. A useful capacity argument

00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:40.446
accounts for the burst duration, consumer
throughput, and acceptable waiting time instead
of

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:42.901
saying that a queue makes the system scale.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.152
An in-process queue is convenient for a thread
pool, but its pending work can disappear when

00:00:48.152 --> 00:00:53.136
the process exits. A separate queue service
outlives an application restart, provided its

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:56.720
own persistence and recovery configuration
support that promise.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.060
A replicated broker adds another failure
boundary and additional coordination. Retained
logs

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.168
add a different property: reading a record does
not necessarily delete it, and consumers

00:01:07.168 --> 00:01:12.508
track their own positions. One analytics
consumer can replay older records without
forcing a

00:01:12.508 --> 00:01:15.120
delivery consumer to repeat the same history.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:20.343
These choices determine recovery behavior and
storage cost. Draw the producer, storage

00:01:20.343 --> 00:01:25.705
boundary, and consumer position explicitly
rather than representing every queue product as

00:01:25.705 --> 00:01:27.374
the same anonymous cylinder.

00:01:27.374 --> 00:01:32.436
Continue accepting work faster than workers can
finish it and a finite queue eventually

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:37.499
fills. At that point the system must delay the
producer, reject new work, shed a lower-

00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:42.504
priority class, or increase usable capacity.
Pretending that acceptance still succeeds

00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:45.413
merely moves the failure to an unknown later
time.

00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:50.621
Monitor the oldest waiting job as well as queue
depth: one hundred very expensive jobs may

00:01:50.621 --> 00:01:55.597
be worse than ten thousand trivial ones. Adding
consumers helps only if the downstream

00:01:55.597 --> 00:02:00.574
database or external provider can support them.
Draw backpressure as a signal from the

00:02:00.574 --> 00:02:04.682
saturated stage toward admission, not as a
mysterious error at the end.

00:02:04.913 --> 00:02:08.559
The goal is to preserve a truthful service
contract under overload.

00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:13.220
An account receives an address change followed
by a shipment request. Even if a queue

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:18.045
delivers them in that order, two workers can
finish the shipment first while the address

00:02:18.045 --> 00:02:23.089
update is still running. Retrying an older
change can create a second ordering problem
after

00:02:23.089 --> 00:02:24.898
newer work has already completed.

00:02:25.059 --> 00:02:30.481
When order matters, choose a scope such as
account identifier and coordinate processing or

00:02:30.481 --> 00:02:35.723
use version checks within that scope. Putting
every account on one globally serial path

00:02:35.723 --> 00:02:41.205
avoids some races but sacrifices unnecessary
parallelism. Repeatedly failing jobs also need

00:02:41.205 --> 00:02:46.266
bounded retries and a controlled recovery path;
otherwise one poison job can consume

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.290
capacity forever.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.211
Make the business invariant determine the
ordering rule, rather than assuming the broker's

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:53.633
terminology guarantees it.
